QCA Cyrus SASL plugin version 2.0.0
-----------------------------------
Date: October 11th, 2007
Website: http://delta.affinix.com/qca/
Mailing List: Delta Project <delta@lists.affinix.com>

Author: Justin Karneges <justin@affinix.com>

This plugin provides features based on Cyrus SASL version 2.

Requirements:
  Cyrus SASL 2.x (libsasl2)

Cryptographic callbacks
-----------------------

When Cyrus SASL exposes SASL_CB_CRYPTO, the plugin supplies hash, HMAC and
PBKDF2 operations through the public QCA API. Algorithm availability follows
the installed QCA providers. SCRAM requires all three operations for its hash;
an algorithm name alone does not guarantee that the mechanism is usable.
The selected backend is authoritative: failures do not fall back to OpenSSL.

A Cyrus library built with --with-crypto=callbacks needs no built-in OpenSSL
backend for SCRAM and the core legacy verifiers. Other Cyrus mechanisms keep
their own dependencies. Older Cyrus versions continue to use their existing
cryptographic implementation. Static OpenSSL-enabled libraries still require
libcrypto, regardless of whether the callback API is present.

Dependency bundles
------------------

The private static dependency bundles contain PLAIN and SCRAM. Their pinned
Cyrus source no longer includes DIGEST-MD5. Mechanisms offered by a separately
installed Cyrus library depend on that library's configuration.

Windows bundles use the CNG backend and require Windows 10 or later. They do
not depend on OpenSSL; cryptographic callbacks still route provider operations
through QCA. Automatic plugin discovery is disabled in these Windows bundles:
initialization uses the embedded mechanisms without consulting registry plugin
paths or loading DLLs from a separately installed Cyrus library. This policy
does not change ordinary shared or static Cyrus builds outside the bundle.
macOS and Android bundles retain the built-in OpenSSL backend.
CNG does not support SHA224, so availability of SCRAM-SHA-224 through callbacks
requires a QCA provider offering the corresponding hash, HMAC and PBKDF2.

Installing
----------

For Unix/Linux/Mac:

  ./configure
  make
  make install

For Windows:

  configwin rd
  qmake
  nmake (or make)
  copy lib\*.dll qtdir\plugins\crypto
